spec.goal:
Define a plan-ready GRACE 4 core/release contract for the grace-marketplace repository.

GRACE 4 replaces the GRACE 3.11 convention bundle with a versioned agent-context platform built around a strict artifact model, a `.grace` project directory, custom XML artifact grammar, deterministic validation, and controlled change lifecycle artifacts.

This spec covers the full GRACE 4 product surface for this repository:
- the `.grace` artifact model and canonical layout;
- the Artifact Grammar and validation rules required for `vv-plan` to plan without inventing core semantics;
- the Bun/TypeScript CLI validation/navigation/status layer;
- canonical skills under `skills/grace/*`;
- packaged skill mirrors under `plugins/grace/skills/grace/*`;
- marketplace/plugin/package metadata and README/release documentation;
- addition of `grace-spec` and `grace-migrate` skills;
- removal of published `grace-multiagent-execute`; parallel-safe execution becomes a mode of `grace-execute`.

Compatibility boundary:
- all new GRACE 4 skills and CLI commands work only with the `.grace` model;
- there is no dual-mode GRACE 3/GRACE 4 support in core skills or CLI;
- GRACE 3 projects enter GRACE 4 only through the `grace-migrate` skill workflow;
- this repository's current operational GRACE 3 `docs/` artifacts are not migrated as part of this GRACE 4 product implementation. The repository remains operationally GRACE 3 until the GRACE 4 release exists, then it can be migrated through the new migration workflow.

Primary outcomes:
- every GRACE 4 XML artifact has a self-describing root tag and `graceVersion` root attribute;
- all semantic anchors remain grep-first XML tags, never attributes;
- durable source-of-truth artifacts are separated from operational change artifacts;
- logical graph and verification projections remain identical regardless of monolithic or segmented physical storage;
- `grace-lint` validates current document state, not lifecycle history;
- skills enforce lifecycle transitions at operation time;
- `grace-execute` is idempotent/recovery-aware without introducing a mandatory persistent run artifact.

spec.tech-stack:
Runtime: Bun/TypeScript, preserving the current `@osovv/grace-cli` package model.

XML parsing and validation:
- use `fast-xml-parser` as the structural XML parser;
- use a custom GRACE Artifact Grammar validator over parsed XML nodes;
- do not use XSD as the source of truth because GRACE requires dynamic semantic tag names;
- do not use regex as a structural parser;
- regex may be used only for local anchor-name pattern recognition, diagnostics, or grep-oriented hints.

Validation responsibilities:
- XML parser: well-formedness, nesting, attributes, CDATA boundaries;
- Artifact Grammar: supported root tags, required sections, allowed root attributes, semantic anchor formats, ownership consistency, status/location invariants, assertions, scopes, cross-links, and projection consistency;
- logical projection builders: combine segmented graph/verification documents into deterministic current-state views.

CLI runtime and compatibility:
- GRACE 4 CLI remains Bun-first;
- Node compatibility is not part of this spec;
- `fast-xml-parser` is added as a runtime dependency;
- CLI commands are `.grace` only, with no dual-mode GRACE 3 validation or navigation.

CLI command surface:
- keep and rewrite for `.grace`: `grace lint`, `grace status`, `grace module`, `grace verification`, `grace file`;
- do not add `grace migrate` as a core CLI command;
- migration is agent-applied through the `grace-migrate` skill;
- CLI may detect a GRACE 3 project only to emit guidance to use `grace-migrate`, not to validate or convert it.

spec.data-flow:
1. Project bootstrap

`grace-init` creates the canonical `.grace` skeleton:
- all five context artifacts;
- `graph/index.xml` and `graph/main.xml`;
- `verification/index.xml` and `verification/main.xml`;
- empty `.grace/changes/active/` and `.grace/changes/archive/` directories.

The project fills context artifacts. `AGENTS.md` becomes the stable bootstrap entrypoint pointing agents to `.grace`, grep-first anchor conventions, skill workflows, CLI availability, and stop/replan rules.

2. Change specification

`grace-spec` interviews the user and creates:

.grace/changes/active/C-.../spec.xml
.grace/changes/active/C-.../design-context.xml   # optional

The spec starts as `GraceChangeSpec status="draft"`. The user explicitly approves, rejects, or cancels it. Approval sets `status="approved"`; rejection/cancellation closes and archives the bundle.

3. Change planning

`grace-plan` accepts only approved `GraceChangeSpec`. It reads current durable context/graph/verification plus optional design context and creates sibling `plan.xml` as `GraceChangePlan status="draft"` with:
- IntentSummary;
- BaselineAssertions;
- TargetAssertions;
- DurableScope;
- ObservedWriteScope;
- ImplementationPlan with `T-*` tasks, dependencies, acceptance criteria, and verification gates.

`grace-plan` surfaces overlap with other active approved plans as strong warnings, not hard blocks. The user explicitly approves, rejects, cancels, or supersedes the plan. Approval sets `status="approved"`.

4. Change execution

`grace-execute` accepts only active bundles where spec and plan are both approved. It performs preflight:
- bundle consistency and matching `C-*`;
- status/location invariants;
- BaselineAssertions against current durable state;
- scope overlap and concurrency safety;
- staleness checks;
- recovery/idempotency classification.

It assesses execution complexity, recommends sequential or parallel-safe mode, and waits for explicit user choice. During implementation, workers may edit only within their allowed ObservedWriteScope. Durable `.grace` updates are applied centrally by the controller after observed work and verification are accepted.

On success:
- implementation is complete;
- fresh verification passes;
- durable graph/verification/context updates are applied;
- TargetAssertions pass;
- full `grace lint` passes;
- spec and plan are marked `status="applied"`;
- the whole bundle is moved to `.grace/changes/archive/C-.../`.

5. Supersede and replan

Approved plans are immutable. If an approved plan proves stale or incorrect during execution, `grace-execute` hard-stops. Replacement of an approved plan requires a new `C-*` bundle. The old bundle is marked `superseded`, references the replacement `C-*`, and is archived.

6. Migration from GRACE 3

`grace-migrate` reads legacy GRACE 3 artifacts such as `docs/requirements.xml`, `docs/technology.xml`, `docs/development-plan.xml`, `docs/knowledge-graph.xml`, `docs/verification-plan.xml`, and `docs/operational-packets.xml` when present. It produces a migration draft/report describing the target `.grace` layout, generated graph/verification ownership, ambiguities, unsupported structures, files to create, and legacy files proposed for removal.

After explicit user approval, the agent writes `.grace` artifacts and validates them. Legacy docs are removed only after valid `.grace` state and explicit cleanup confirmation. Failed migration leaves legacy artifacts untouched. No retroactive `C-*` bundles are created.

7. Validation and status

`grace-lint` parses `.grace` XML through `fast-xml-parser`, applies Artifact Grammar validation, builds graph and verification projections, validates current-state lifecycle/location invariants, evaluates static assertion consistency where applicable, and reports structured diagnostics.

`grace-status` summarizes durable health, operational activity, derived conflicts, stale plans, and drift. It distinguishes explained active-change contexts from unexplained observed drift.

spec.error-handling:
General validation errors:
- unknown root tag, missing `graceVersion`, unsupported `graceVersion`, malformed XML, forbidden semantic-anchor attributes, duplicate anchors, dangling references, missing ownership routes, ownership mismatch, invalid status, invalid status location, or missing required sections produce explicit diagnostic codes and file-localized messages where possible.

Change lifecycle:
- `grace-plan` refuses draft, rejected, cancelled, applied, superseded, or missing specs;
- `grace-execute` refuses missing plans, non-approved specs/plans, already closed bundles, mismatched `C-*`, invalid status/location, failed BaselineAssertions, unsafe concurrent overlap, or missing explicit execution mode choice;
- rejected/cancelled/superseded bundles must be archived and must not remain active;
- superseded bundles require a replacement `C-*` reference.

Approved plan immutability:
- `grace-execute` must hard-stop if an approved plan is incorrect, stale, contradictory, or missing necessary dependency/scope/verification data;
- it must not silently edit an approved plan;
- replacing an approved plan requires a new `C-*` bundle.

Execution recovery:
- `clean-to-start` may proceed after normal confirmation;
- `partial-observed-writes` may continue only if the observed state is explainable, within ObservedWriteScope, and compatible with TargetAssertions;
- `durable-state-changed` requires baseline re-evaluation and may hard-stop as stale;
- `target-already-satisfied` may proceed to durable apply only after fresh verification and TargetAssertions pass;
- `unsafe-unknown-drift` hard-stops and requires rollback, reconciliation, cancellation, or superseding change.

Parallel-safe execution:
- hard-stop if two workers would write overlapping ObservedWriteScope files/globs;
- hard-stop if concurrent execution would overlap durable scope unsafely;
- hard-stop if a worker writes outside its bounded ObservedWriteScope;
- only the controller may apply durable `.grace` updates.

Migration safety:
- `grace-migrate` must present a migration draft/report before writing;
- destructive cleanup requires valid `.grace` state and explicit confirmation;
- if git is unavailable or worktree state is not clean, migration requires explicit acknowledgement before destructive cleanup;
- failure leaves legacy GRACE 3 artifacts untouched;
- no CLI command may silently convert or delete legacy docs.

Project context applicability:
- missing mandatory context artifacts are validation errors;
- `deployment` and `ux-guidelines` may be `not-applicable` only with a real reason;
- lack of a web UI is not sufficient to mark UX not applicable.

testing.coverage:
Artifact Grammar fixtures:
- minimal valid `.grace` project;
- missing `graceVersion`;
- unsupported `graceVersion`;
- invalid root tag;
- forbidden semantic-anchor attribute usage;
- duplicate `M-*`;
- duplicate `V-M-*`;
- unlisted graph anchor in document;
- listed but missing graph anchor;
- `GD-*` index mismatch with document wrapper;
- same mismatch coverage for `VD-*`.

Graph/verification projection fixtures:
- monolithic graph projection;
- segmented graph projection equivalent to monolithic;
- lossless merge equivalence;
- cross-document link validity;
- dangling graph and verification references;
- deterministic `M-*` to `V-M-*` coverage.

Change lifecycle fixtures:
- draft spec only;
- approved spec with missing plan;
- approved spec with draft plan;
- approved spec and approved plan executable;
- rejected/cancelled/superseded bundles archived;
- invalid closed status in active;
- invalid open status in archive;
- superseded without replacement reference;
- overlapping approved plans allowed but surfaced;
- parallel-safe overlap blocks unsafe execution.

Assertion fixtures:
- `MustExist`;
- `MustNotExist`;
- `MustOwn`;
- `MustLink`;
- `MustVerify`;
- `MustPassCommand`;
- `MustContain`;
- `MustNotContain`;
- stale BaselineAssertions after durable state changes.

Recovery fixtures:
- clean-to-start;
- partial observed writes;
- durable-state-changed;
- target-already-satisfied;
- unsafe-unknown-drift.

Migration fixtures:
- valid GRACE 3 project converted to valid `.grace` by agent-applied workflow;
- missing optional v3 docs handled in migration draft/report;
- invalid or ambiguous v3 docs produce report and hard-stop;
- legacy docs are not deleted before valid `.grace`;
- no retroactive `C-*` bundles are created.

CLI fixtures:
- `grace lint` validates `.grace` and rejects invalid current state;
- `grace status` reports derived overlap/stale/drift states without mutating XML;
- `grace module` resolves `M-*` through graph index/projection;
- `grace verification` resolves `V-M-*` through verification index/projection;
- `grace file` reports file-local/private semantic markup and artifact-local anchors;
- CLI detects GRACE 3 only to emit migration guidance, not to enter dual mode.

Repository packaging fixtures:
- canonical skills and packaged mirrors are synchronized;
- marketplace manifest includes GRACE 4 skill surface;
- `grace-multiagent-execute` is absent from published manifest;
- `grace-spec` and `grace-migrate` are present in published manifest;
- versions are synchronized across package metadata, marketplace metadata, plugin metadata, README, and openpackage metadata;
- `fast-xml-parser` dependency is present;
- release validation runs `bun test`, GRACE 4 CLI validation, and marketplace validation scripts.
